# Git-layer line-ending policy. Mirrors .editorconfig — which editors honor
# but git does not — so the rule is also enforced on checkout and commit.
#
# LF is the committed form for every text file. GitHub's windows-latest
# runner defaults core.autocrlf=true, which would otherwise give a Windows
# checkout a CRLF working tree.
#
# The cross-platform `lwpt install --frozen` digests are made
# line-ending-independent IN CODE, not by this file: HashTree normalizes
# content CRLF->LF and pins its fold order (TreeHashPathCompare), and the
# constraint fingerprint pins its separator and line sort. So a CRLF
# checkout verifies regardless of what git does here. This rule is
# defense-in-depth and plain hygiene — keep the working tree LF so
# committed bytes, editor output, and the digest inputs never disagree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows shells require CRLF (mirrors .editorconfig's [*.{bat,cmd}]/[*.ps1]).
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Exact-byte fixtures git must never line-ending-normalize: PKCS#12 TLS
# identities are binary, and the registry .hex objects are named by the
# sha256 of their own bytes — a rewritten byte would corrupt the fixture.
*.p12 binary
*.hex binary
